<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/068R">Remark <span data-tag="068R">9.3.2.23</span>.</a> The conclusion of Corollary <a data-tag="0684" href="/tag/0684">9.3.2.22</a> is also valid in the case $\kappa = \aleph _0$, provided that we follow the convention that $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{&lt; \aleph _0}$ denotes the full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ spanned by its compact objects: that is, the full subcategory spanned by the finitely dominated Kan complexes (Proposition <a data-tag="067X" href="/tag/067X">9.2.6.7</a>). Note that this is a slightly different assertion than Proposition <a data-tag="06FT" href="/tag/06FT">9.3.2.20</a>, because the inclusion $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{\mathrm{fin}} \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}_{ &lt; \aleph _0 }$ is not an equivalence of $\infty $-categories. This is a special case of a more general phenomenon: a Morita equivalence of $\infty $-categories induces an equivalence between their $\operatorname{Ind}$-completions. See Exercise <a data-tag="067H" href="/tag/067H">9.3.2.7</a>. </p>
